# 1. Component lifecycle hooks

## 1.1 What is the component lifecycle?

Every Angular component passes through a sequence of stages:

1. Angular creates the component instance.
2. Angular initializes its input properties.
3. Angular initializes its template and child components.
4. Angular checks the component for updates during change detection.
5. Angular eventually destroys the component when it is removed from the view.

Lifecycle hooks are methods that allow developers to execute code at specific stages of this process. They are useful when a component needs to initialize data, react to input changes, access its view, or clean up resources.&#x20;

Consider our Product Management application. A product-details component might need to load or prepare information when created, react when the selected product changes, access an input element after rendering, and clean up a subscription when the user navigates away.

Different lifecycle hooks support these different responsibilities.

## 1.2 Lifecycle hook execution order

1\. Constructor

JavaScript object construction and basic dependency injection

2\. `ngOnChanges()`

Runs when component inputs change; first invocation occurs during initialization

3\. `ngOnInit()`

Runs once after initial input values are available

4\. Content and view initialization

`ngAfterContentInit()` and `ngAfterViewInit()`

5\. Subsequent checks and rendering

Input changes, template checks, content/view checks, render callbacks

6\. `ngOnDestroy()`

Clean up resources before the component is destroyed

This is a simplified sequence, not every hook that Angular invokes. For example, `ngDoCheck()`, `ngAfterContentChecked()`, and `ngAfterViewChecked()` can run repeatedly during checks. `afterNextRender()` and `afterEveryRender()` are render callbacks that have different timing from instance lifecycle methods.

## 1.3 Important lifecycle hooks

| Hook                      | When it runs                                | Typical use                                      |
| ------------------------- | ------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------ |
| `ngOnChanges()`           | When an input changes                       | React to a newly selected product                |
| `ngOnInit()`              | Once after initial inputs are initialized   | Perform initial component setup                  |
| `ngDoCheck()`             | During each check of the component          | Specialized custom change checking               |
| `ngAfterContentInit()`    | Once after projected content initializes    | Access content queries                           |
| `ngAfterContentChecked()` | After projected content is checked          | Specialized content-check handling               |
| `ngAfterViewInit()`       | Once after the component's view initializes | Access initialized view queries                  |
| `ngAfterViewChecked()`    | After the component's view is checked       | Specialized view-check handling                  |
| `afterNextRender()`       | After the next application render           | Integrate with DOM-dependent operations          |
| `afterEveryRender()`      | After every application render              | Perform carefully controlled render-related work |
| `ngOnDestroy()`           | Before the instance is destroyed            | Clean up resources                               |

Avoid using `ngDoCheck()`, `ngAfterContentChecked()`, or `ngAfterViewChecked()` for ordinary application logic; they can execute frequently and affect performance.&#x20;

### 1.4 `constructor()` versus `ngOnInit()`

Java developers may be familiar with constructors, but a constructor and an Angular lifecycle hook serve different purposes.

The constructor creates the JavaScript object and can receive dependencies through Angular's dependency injection.

`ngOnInit()` runs after Angular has initialized the component's initial inputs. This makes it suitable for initialization that depends on those values.

```
import { Component, OnInit, input } from '@angular/core';

@Component({
    selector: 'app-product-summary',
    standalone: true,
    template: `<h2>{{ productName() }}</h2>`
})
export class ProductSummaryComponent implements OnInit {

    productName = input.required<string>();

    constructor() {
        console.log('Component instance constructed');
    }

    ngOnInit(): void {
        console.log('Initial product:', this.productName());
    }
}
```

When the component is created, Angular assigns the input before calling `ngOnInit()`. Thus, `ngOnInit()` can use the initial value of `productName`.

Best practice: Use the constructor for construction and dependency injection. Use `ngOnInit()` for initialization that requires Angular's initialized inputs.

## 1.5 `ngOnChanges()` — reacting to a selected product

Suppose the product-details component receives a product from its parent. The parent might select Laptop first and then select Monitor.

The component should react when its input changes.

```
import {
    Component,
    input,
    OnChanges,
    SimpleChanges
} from '@angular/core';

import { Product } from './product.model';

@Component({
    selector: 'app-product-details',
    standalone: true,
    template: `
        <h2>{{ product().name }}</h2>
        <p>Price: ₹{{ product().price }}</p>
    `
})
export class ProductDetailsComponent implements OnChanges {

    product = input.required<Product>();

    ngOnChanges(
        changes: SimpleChanges<ProductDetailsComponent>
    ): void {

        const change = changes.product;

        if (change) {
            console.log('Previous product:', change.previousValue);
            console.log('Current product:', change.currentValue);
            console.log('First change:', change.firstChange);
        }
    }
}
```

`ngOnChanges()` receives a `SimpleChanges` object. Each entry describes a changed input, including its previous value, current value, and whether it is the first change.

The first `ngOnChanges()` call occurs before `ngOnInit()` when the component has initialized inputs. Subsequent calls occur when inputs change.&#x20;

An important limitation: this hook observes changes to the input value or reference. Mutating a property inside an existing object without replacing the object reference is not the same as assigning a new input value.

For our product demo, we will select a different product object to demonstrate input changes.

## 1.6 `ngAfterViewInit()` — accessing the initialized view

Suppose we need to focus the product search field after the component's view has initialized.

A modern Angular approach is to use a view query and `ngAfterViewInit()`.

```
import {
    AfterViewInit,
    Component,
    ElementRef,
    ViewChild
} from '@angular/core';

@Component({
    selector: 'app-product-search',
    standalone: true,
    template: `
        <label for="search">Product search</label>
        <input #searchBox id="search" type="search">
    `
})
export class ProductSearchComponent implements AfterViewInit {

    @ViewChild('searchBox')
    searchBox!: ElementRef<HTMLInputElement>;

    ngAfterViewInit(): void {
        this.searchBox.nativeElement.focus();
    }
}
```

Angular resolves the view query, and `ngAfterViewInit()` runs once after the component's view has been initialized.

This example demonstrates the lifecycle concept, but direct DOM interaction should be used carefully. In SSR applications, browser-only operations must be handled in an appropriate browser context. For DOM work that specifically needs to happen after rendering, Angular's render callbacks, such as `afterNextRender()`, may be more suitable.

## 1.7 `ngOnDestroy()` — cleanup

A component might create a timer, subscribe to a long-lived stream, or register an event listener.

When the component is destroyed, resources that are no longer needed must be cleaned up.

```
import {
    Component,
    OnDestroy,
    OnInit
} from '@angular/core';

@Component({
    selector: 'app-product-monitor',
    standalone: true,
    template: `<p>Product monitor is active.</p>`
})
export class ProductMonitorComponent implements OnInit, OnDestroy {

    private timerId?: ReturnType<typeof setInterval>;

    ngOnInit(): void {
        this.timerId = setInterval(() => {
            console.log('Checking product status...');
        }, 5000);
    }

    ngOnDestroy(): void {
        if (this.timerId !== undefined) {
            clearInterval(this.timerId);
        }

        console.log('Product monitor destroyed');
    }
}
```

This is a simple demonstration. In a real application, a backend-driven stock-monitoring mechanism would usually be implemented through an appropriate service or stream rather than a console timer.

Angular also provides `DestroyRef` and RxJS utilities such as `takeUntilDestroyed()` to simplify cleanup in suitable contexts.&#x20;

