# 2. View encapsulation

## 2.1 What is view encapsulation?

View encapsulation determines how Angular scopes a component's CSS and how those styles interact with elements outside the component.

For a Product Management application, imagine that:

- The product list uses a blue border.
- A low-stock product card uses an orange border.
- The product-details component has its own padding and typography.

Without a clear styling strategy, CSS selectors could conflict across different parts of the application.

Angular supports multiple view encapsulation modes. The default is `ViewEncapsulation.Emulated`. Other important modes include `ShadowDom` and `None`.&#x20;


## 2.2 Mode 1: `ViewEncapsulation.Emulated`

This is Angular's default mode.

Angular scopes component styles by generating special attributes and adjusting CSS selectors. This approximates the isolation expected from Shadow DOM without creating a native shadow root.

```
import {
    Component,
    ViewEncapsulation
} from '@angular/core';

@Component({
    selector: 'app-product-card',
    standalone: true,
    template: `
        <article class="product-card">
            <h3>Laptop</h3>
            <p>Price: ₹65,000</p>
        </article>
    `,
    styles: `
        .product-card {
            padding: 16px;
            border: 2px solid green;
            border-radius: 8px;
        }

        h3 {
            color: darkgreen;
        }
    `,
    encapsulation: ViewEncapsulation.Emulated
})
export class ProductCardComponent {}
```

The component's styles are scoped to the component's template. Angular achieves this by adding generated attributes to elements and adjusting the corresponding selectors.

### Important distinction

`Emulated` does not mean complete CSS isolation. Global styles can still affect elements inside the component, so specificity and global CSS conventions remain important.

Use this mode when developing ordinary Angular application components. It gives useful style scoping without the broader behavioural implications of native Shadow DOM.

## 2.3 Mode 2: `ViewEncapsulation.ShadowDom`

This mode uses the browser's native Shadow DOM API.

```
import {
    Component,
    ViewEncapsulation
} from '@angular/core';

@Component({
    selector: 'app-product-card',
    standalone: true,
    template: `
        <article class="product-card">
            <h3>Laptop</h3>
            <p>Price: ₹65,000</p>
        </article>
    `,
    styles: `
        .product-card {
            padding: 16px;
            border: 2px solid blue;
            border-radius: 8px;
        }

        h3 {
            color: navy;
        }
    `,
    encapsulation: ViewEncapsulation.ShadowDom
})
export class ProductCardComponent {}
```

Angular creates a shadow root for the component and renders its template and styles inside that shadow tree.

This provides stronger CSS isolation from ordinary external selectors.

However, Shadow DOM affects more than styling. It also introduces considerations for event propagation, slots, DOM inspection, and integration with global styles.

Use it when genuine encapsulation is required and the implications of the browser's Shadow DOM model are understood.

## 2.4 Mode 3: `ViewEncapsulation.None`

With this mode, Angular does not scope the component's CSS.

```
import {
    Component,
    ViewEncapsulation
} from '@angular/core';

@Component({
    selector: 'app-product-card',
    standalone: true,
    template: `
        <article class="product-card">
            <h3>Laptop</h3>
        </article>
    `,
    styles: `
        .product-card {
            border: 2px solid red;
        }
    `,
    encapsulation: ViewEncapsulation.None
})
export class ProductCardComponent {}
```

The `.product-card` rule behaves like a global CSS rule. It can affect matching elements outside this component, including unrelated components that use the same class name.

This mode can be useful for deliberate global styling, but it should not be used casually. Reusable components often need to avoid leaking styles into the rest of the application.

## 2.5 Comparison of view encapsulation modes

| Mode        | CSS scoping                                                  | Native Shadow DOM | Typical use                                 |
| ----------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ | ----------------- | ------------------------------------------- |
| `Emulated`  | Angular-generated scoping                                    | No                | Normal Angular application components       |
| `ShadowDom` | Browser-enforced shadow-tree boundary for ordinary selectors | Yes               | Components requiring native style isolation |
| `None`      | No component style isolation                                 | No                | Intentional global styling                  |

Angular also documents `ExperimentalIsolatedShadowDom`, an experimental mode that prevents global styles from affecting the component's shadow tree. It should not be confused with the three primary modes above.&#x20;

### Practical styling recommendation

For our Product Management application, use `Emulated` encapsulation for most feature components. Use global styles for shared design tokens, typography, and resets. Avoid `None` unless global styling is intentional, and introduce Shadow DOM only when its encapsulation benefits justify its integration costs.

# 3. Change detection basics

## 3.1 What is change detection?

Change detection is the process Angular uses to determine whether values used by templates have changed and whether corresponding DOM updates are necessary.

Suppose the Product Management page displays:

```
<h2>{{ product.name }}</h2>
<p>Available stock: {{ product.stockQuantity }}</p>
```

When the application changes the displayed product or its stock quantity, Angular needs to reconcile the template with the current state.

In broad terms, Angular traverses relevant portions of the component tree, evaluates bindings, and updates the DOM where necessary.

Understanding this process helps developers avoid stale UI, unnecessary work, and incorrect assumptions about object mutation.

## 3.2 `Eager` versus `OnPush`

Angular's change-detection strategy determines how components are checked.

The current Angular 22 API documents `Eager` and `OnPush` as the two strategies, with `OnPush` enabled by default for new Angular applications. `Default` is deprecated in Angular 22 as an alias for `Eager`. Older Angular versions commonly defaulted to the eager/default strategy, so version differences matter.&#x20;

### Eager change detection

With eager change detection, Angular checks a component when the change-detection traversal reaches it after relevant application activity.

This is conceptually similar to the traditional default behaviour familiar to developers from older Angular versions.

### OnPush change detection

With `OnPush`, Angular can skip checking a component subtree when there is no relevant notification that the subtree needs to be checked.

Important notifications include:

- A changed input received through a template binding.
- An event listener running in the component.
- A Signal read by the template changing.
- Explicit marking through `ChangeDetectorRef.markForCheck()`.
- Other supported Angular notifications, such as those generated by `AsyncPipe`.

`OnPush` is not simply a manual switch that turns change detection off forever. It is a more selective change-detection strategy.&#x20;

## 3.3 Product Management example using Signals

Let us implement a small inventory component.

```
import {
    ChangeDetectionStrategy,
    Component,
    computed,
    signal
} from '@angular/core';

@Component({
    selector: 'app-inventory',
    standalone: true,
    changeDetection: ChangeDetectionStrategy.OnPush,
    template: `
        <h2>Inventory</h2>

        <p>Available stock: {{ stockQuantity() }}</p>
        <p>Status: {{ stockStatus() }}</p>

        <button
            [disabled]="stockQuantity() === 0"
            (click)="sellOneUnit()">

            Sell one unit
        </button>
    `
})
export class InventoryComponent {

    stockQuantity = signal(10);

    stockStatus = computed(() =>
        this.stockQuantity() === 0
            ? 'Out of stock'
            : this.stockQuantity() <= 5
                ? 'Low stock'
                : 'In stock'
    );

    sellOneUnit(): void {
        this.stockQuantity.update(quantity =>
            Math.max(0, quantity - 1)
        );
    }
}
```

When Sell one unit is clicked:

1. Angular invokes `sellOneUnit()`.
2. The writable Signal is updated.
3. `stockStatus`, the computed Signal, derives the new status.
4. Angular sees that the template depends on the changed Signal.
5. The component is marked for checking and its rendered state is updated accordingly.

This is a good fit for an `OnPush` component. The same concept also works with the `OnPush` default in current Angular.

## 3.4 Why immutable updates matter

Consider a common mistake when updating a product collection.

Suppose a component maintains a Signal:

```
products = signal<Product[]>([
    {
        id: 101,
        name: 'Laptop',
        price: 65000,
        stockQuantity: 12
    }
]);
```

The following update mutates the object inside the array:

```
this.products()[0].stockQuantity = 5;
```

This modifies a nested property but does not call `set()` or `update()` on the Signal. The Signal itself has not been notified of a new value.

A better approach is to create a new array and a new object for the changed product:

```
updateStock(productId: number, stockQuantity: number): void {
    this.products.update(products =>
        products.map(product =>
            product.id === productId
                ? { ...product, stockQuantity }
                : product
        )
    );
}
```

This version:

- Creates a new array.
- Creates a new object for the updated product.
- Leaves unaffected product objects unchanged.
- Notifies consumers of the Signal that its value has changed.

Immutable updates are especially helpful when state is passed through inputs, used by Signals, or consumed by `OnPush` components.

They are not an absolute requirement for every property mutation, but they make state transitions more predictable and easier to reason about.

## 3.5 What is `ChangeDetectorRef`?

`ChangeDetectorRef` provides APIs for explicitly interacting with Angular's change-detection system.

For example:

```
import {
    ChangeDetectorRef,
    Component,
    inject
} from '@angular/core';

@Component({
    selector: 'app-product-counter',
    standalone: true,
    template: `<p>Products: {{ productCount }}</p>`
})
export class ProductCounterComponent {

    private cdr = inject(ChangeDetectorRef);

    productCount = 0;

    refreshCount(count: number): void {
        this.productCount = count;
        this.cdr.markForCheck();
    }
}
```

`markForCheck()` marks the component so that Angular checks it during a subsequent change-detection pass.

In this simple example, a normal Angular event or reactive state mechanism may already provide the necessary notification. The method is more relevant when integrating external callbacks or other update sources that Angular does not automatically track through the component's normal notification mechanisms.

Other methods, such as `detectChanges()`, `detach()`, and `reattach()`, offer more explicit control. They should be used only when necessary because they can make rendering behaviour more complicated.

## 3.6 Angular Signals and modern change detection

Signals work particularly well with `OnPush` components.

When a Signal is read in a component's template, Angular records that dependency. If the Signal changes, Angular marks the component so it can be updated.

For our Product Management application, this means a product stock update can refresh the relevant UI without requiring a developer to manually call `detectChanges()` after every Signal update.&#x20;

One more version-specific point: zoneless change detection became the default in Angular 21 and later. Modern Angular relies on notifications from Angular APIs such as Signals, template listeners, and `markForCheck()` rather than requiring ZoneJS to identify every asynchronous activity.&#x20;

# 4. Complete demonstration: Product Details Lifecycle

We will now combine lifecycle hooks, view encapsulation, and change detection into a single demo.

The parent will display a product selector. Selecting another product will update the child's input. Clearing the selection will destroy the child component.

## Step 1: Create the model

File: `product.model.ts`

```
export interface Product {
    id: number;
    name: string;
    price: number;
    stockQuantity: number;
}
```

## Step 2: Create the details component

File: `product-details.ts`

```
import {
    ChangeDetectionStrategy,
    Component,
    input,
    OnChanges,
    OnDestroy,
    OnInit,
    SimpleChanges,
    ViewEncapsulation
} from '@angular/core';

import { Product } from './product.model';

@Component({
    selector: 'app-product-details',
    standalone: true,
    changeDetection: ChangeDetectionStrategy.OnPush,
    encapsulation: ViewEncapsulation.Emulated,
    template: `
        <article class="product-detail">
            <h2>{{ product().name }}</h2>

            <p>Product ID: {{ product().id }}</p>
            <p>Price: ₹{{ product().price }}</p>
            <p>Stock: {{ product().stockQuantity }}</p>

            @if (product().stockQuantity === 0) {
                <p class="out-of-stock">Out of stock</p>
            } @else if (product().stockQuantity <= 5) {
                <p class="low-stock">Low stock</p>
            } @else {
                <p class="available">In stock</p>
            }
        </article>
    `,
    styles: `
        :host {
            display: block;
            margin-top: 20px;
        }

        .product-detail {
            padding: 20px;
            border: 1px solid #cbd5e1;
            border-radius: 8px;
            background: white;
        }

        .low-stock {
            color: #b45309;
            font-weight: bold;
        }

        .out-of-stock {
            color: #b91c1c;
            font-weight: bold;
        }

        .available {
            color: #15803d;
        }
    `
})
export class ProductDetailsComponent
    implements OnChanges, OnInit, OnDestroy {

    product = input.required<Product>();

    ngOnChanges(
        changes: SimpleChanges<ProductDetailsComponent>
    ): void {
        const productChange = changes.product;

        if (productChange) {
            console.log('ngOnChanges:', {
                previous: productChange.previousValue?.name,
                current: productChange.currentValue?.name,
                firstChange: productChange.firstChange
            });
        }
    }

    ngOnInit(): void {
        console.log(
            'ngOnInit: Product details initialized for',
            this.product().name
        );
    }

    ngOnDestroy(): void {
        console.log(
            'ngOnDestroy: Product details removed from the view'
        );
    }
}
```

This component demonstrates three important ideas:

- `ngOnChanges()` responds to a changed product input.
- `ngOnInit()` runs once for each instance.
- `ngOnDestroy()` runs when the component is removed from the view.

It also uses `ViewEncapsulation.Emulated` and `ChangeDetectionStrategy.OnPush`. The CSS rules define the component's visual presentation, and the Signal-based input supplies the displayed product.

## Step 3: Create the parent product selector

File: `product-page.ts`

```
import {
    Component,
    signal
} from '@angular/core';

import { Product } from './product.model';
import { ProductDetailsComponent } from './product-details';

@Component({
    selector: 'app-product-page',
    standalone: true,
    imports: [ProductDetailsComponent],
    template: `
        <h1>Product Management</h1>

        <h3>Select a product</h3>

        <div class="product-buttons">
            @for (product of products; track product.id) {
                <button
                    type="button"
                    (click)="selectProduct(product)">

                    {{ product.name }}
                </button>
            }
        </div>

        <button
            type="button"
            (click)="clearSelection()">

            Clear selection
        </button>

        @if (selectedProduct(); as product) {
            <app-product-details [product]="product" />
        } @else {
            <p>Select a product to view its details.</p>
        }
    `,
    styles: `
        .product-buttons {
            display: flex;
            flex-wrap: wrap;
            gap: 10px;
            margin: 16px 0;
        }

        button {
            padding: 10px 14px;
            cursor: pointer;
        }
    `
})
export class ProductPageComponent {

    products: Product[] = [
        {
            id: 101,
            name: 'Laptop',
            price: 65000,
            stockQuantity: 12
        },
        {
            id: 102,
            name: 'Keyboard',
            price: 1500,
            stockQuantity: 4
        },
        {
            id: 103,
            name: 'Monitor',
            price: 12000,
            stockQuantity: 0
        }
    ];

    selectedProduct = signal<Product | null>(null);

    selectProduct(product: Product): void {
        this.selectedProduct.set(product);
    }

    clearSelection(): void {
        this.selectedProduct.set(null);
    }
}
```

## Step 4: Display the page from the root component

Import `ProductPageComponent` into your application's root component.

For example:

```
import { Component } from '@angular/core';
import { ProductPageComponent } from './features/products/product-page';

@Component({
    selector: 'app-root',
    standalone: true,
    imports: [ProductPageComponent],
    template: `<app-product-page />`
})
export class AppComponent {}
```

Adjust the import paths to match the actual file locations in your workspace.

## Step 5: Run the demonstration

```
ng serve
```

Open the local application in your browser and select Laptop, Keyboard, and Monitor in sequence.

Then click Clear selection.

### Expected lifecycle behaviour

| User action                             | Expected behaviour                                                                |
| --------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| The page loads with no product selected | The details component is not created                                              |
| The user selects Laptop                 | A details instance is created; initial input change and initialization hooks run  |
| The user selects Keyboard               | The existing details instance receives a new input, so `ngOnChanges()` runs again |
| The user selects Monitor                | The same details instance updates to the new product                              |
| The user clicks Clear selection         | The details component is removed, and `ngOnDestroy()` runs                        |

This demonstrates how the component lifecycle relates to ordinary user interactions. It also shows how Signals and `OnPush` can work together to keep a child component's displayed data updated.

# 5. Best practices

| Area                  | Recommended practice                                                                                        |
| --------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Lifecycle hooks       | Use `ngOnInit()` for initialization and `ngOnDestroy()` or `DestroyRef` for cleanup                         |
| Input updates         | Use `ngOnChanges()` when behaviour depends on input changes                                                 |
| DOM access            | Prefer Angular's template and query APIs; use render callbacks when DOM work needs to occur after rendering |
| Performance           | Avoid expensive operations in hooks that run on every check                                                 |
| CSS                   | Use the default `Emulated` encapsulation for most application components                                    |
| Change detection      | Understand `OnPush`, immutable updates, and Signals                                                         |
| Manual detection      | Reserve `ChangeDetectorRef` operations for cases that genuinely need them                                   |
| Version compatibility | Confirm the Angular version before relying on defaults, as Angular 22 uses `OnPush` by default              |


